WEBVTT

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:09.740
In the previous video we used the
spacebar to make the character jump.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.226
But in this video we want to create a more
of a perfect jump so we're going to be able

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:18.680
to modify the jump force and the gravity
to make it feel a little bit more snappy.

00:00:18.681 --> 00:00:21.629
If we head back into
our player controller script

00:00:21.641 --> 00:00:24.240
and we never get down
to the update method.

00:00:24.241 --> 00:00:29.080
Using a value in the advance of 10 and it's
not great to add code the value down here.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.159
What we want to do is
add a brand new variable.

00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:35.171
So we'll call this one
public float and we'll just

00:00:35.172 --> 00:00:38.840
call this jump force and
we'll set that equal to 10.

00:00:38.841 --> 00:00:40.160
Just like we had before.

00:00:40.161 --> 00:00:42.845
Now we can copy our
jump force and replace that

00:00:42.857 --> 00:00:45.440
times 10 in there just
so that we can control

00:00:45.614 --> 00:00:46.840
it in the inspector.

00:00:46.841 --> 00:00:48.423
In our start method
we want to be able to

00:00:48.435 --> 00:00:50.185
control that gravity
to make it feel a little

00:00:50.209 --> 00:00:55.160
bit more snappy while bringing the player
back down to where it should be originally.

00:00:55.161 --> 00:00:58.160
So what we can do is use physics.

00:00:58.161 --> 00:01:03.080
Which is just the physics system and we can
use dot and then we'll say gravity because

00:01:03.081 --> 00:01:05.174
we can affect gravity
on all rigid bodies

00:01:05.186 --> 00:01:07.600
within the scene and
we can use the star or the

00:01:07.601 --> 00:01:13.280
multiply symbol equals a variable
that we're going to be able to use.

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:15.760
I'll just name that variable.

00:01:15.761 --> 00:01:18.162
Now up here we want
to have another public

00:01:18.174 --> 00:01:20.760
float and the variable
we're going to create.

00:01:20.761 --> 00:01:25.160
Call this one gravity modifier and you don't
need to give it an initial value if you

00:01:25.161 --> 00:01:28.765
don't want and I'm just
going to copy that and paste

00:01:28.865 --> 00:01:33.360
that in here and make sure I
add a semicolon on the end.

00:01:33.361 --> 00:01:36.440
This means we're just
going to multiply the

00:01:36.452 --> 00:01:39.760
gravity that we have
by the gravity multiplier

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:46.160
and that multiplication equals is just a
shorthand way of saying physics dot gravity

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:53.160
equals physics dot gravity
times by gravity modifier.

00:01:53.161 --> 00:01:56.415
So you can see we've
just removed this portion

00:01:56.427 --> 00:01:59.480
like this and we still
have that multiplier

00:01:59.481 --> 00:02:02.929
and that equals and we
just move it multiply over

00:02:02.941 --> 00:02:06.120
there and we can then
get the same statement.

00:02:06.145 --> 00:02:09.360
It just makes it a shorthand
version of doing the same thing.

00:02:09.361 --> 00:02:11.464
If we save this script
we go back into unity

00:02:11.476 --> 00:02:16.600
select on our player and you can see the
jump forces 10 and gravity modifier is zero.

00:02:16.601 --> 00:02:18.522
Now if we test this
out something weird is

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:20.560
going to happen if we
press base you can see

00:02:20.561 --> 00:02:24.520
that there is no gravity and
they just character flies up forever.

00:02:24.521 --> 00:02:27.128
So if we stop that and
you can see if we set

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.640
the gravity modifier
to one this will just

00:02:29.641 --> 00:02:34.440
be whatever gravity is times one
so that will be just its default value.

00:02:34.441 --> 00:02:37.400
So if we press play again it
will be just like we did originally.

00:02:37.401 --> 00:02:39.632
Just remember each
time you want to modify the

00:02:39.644 --> 00:02:41.840
gravity modifier while
you're in game because

00:02:41.841 --> 00:02:44.319
it only does it once
in the start method so

00:02:44.331 --> 00:02:48.680
make sure you just stop your
game and modify that value after.

00:02:48.681 --> 00:02:51.090
So I've stopped my
game, I've set the jump

00:02:51.102 --> 00:02:55.600
force 15 and then modified it to and
now the jump looks a little bit snappier.

00:02:55.601 --> 00:02:59.880
So what I want you to be able to do is go
back into your player controller script, add

00:02:59.881 --> 00:03:05.480
yourself a public float for jump force and
gravity modifier, set the jump force equal

00:03:05.505 --> 00:03:09.038
to 10 and then within
your start method use

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:18.840
physics dot gravity multiplied
equals gravity modifier.